# Enter the Numerical Bestiary

Begin with the smallest specimen: **1260 = 21 × 60**. The digits are conserved, but a hidden congruence is doing the real work. Try examples directly in the inspector.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

## The six decimal gates

Every decimal fang pair lies on `(x−1)(y−1) ≡ 1 (mod 9)`. The next widget displays this curve and lets you change the positional base.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the digit-sum argument</summary>
In base `b`, every power of `b` is `1` modulo `b−1`, so a number equals its digit sum modulo `b−1`. Exact digit-multiset equality gives `xy ≡ x+y`; adding one after rearrangement yields `(x−1)(y−1) ≡ 1`.
</details>

## Compute the invariant

Use the modular partner-residue algorithm before any expensive digit comparison.

{{algorithm:0}}

Then apply the exact frequency-vector test.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:0}}

## Across positional bases

The curve contains exactly one point for each unit modulo `b−1`, hence `φ(b−1)` points. Explore the changing count and proportion.

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the unit correspondence</summary>
Put `u=X−1`. A curve point makes `u` invertible and uniquely determines `Y−1=u⁻¹`. Conversely every unit supplies one point. See [Euler's totient function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler%27s_totient_function) and [modular arithmetic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_arithmetic) for background.
</details>

## The prime-fang gate

Primality removes the three points divisible by three. The survivors are `(2,2)`, `(5,8)`, and `(8,5)`, all with product residue `4`.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the prime argument</summary>
A prime divisible by `3` must equal `3`; the exceptional case cannot complete one of the discarded curve points with another prime. Multiplying each surviving residue pair gives `4 mod 9`.
</details>

## Run a bounded search

The complete elementary pipeline factors each candidate, applies the unit-curve sieve, and compares digit frequencies. Additional equal-length and trailing-zero rules select classical vampire numbers.

{{algorithm:2}}

For broader context, read about [vampire numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vampire_number). Density and interval-existence claims remain open here: modular admissibility is necessary, not sufficient.

